  "query_preview": "..\n\nVery warm today with a record high already broken at FNT, based on a \n20Z obs of 85F, but even warmer readings exist further northwest as \nIKW, MBS, and HYX log readings of 88F, 86F, and 86F, respectively.\n\nAn active period of weather gets underway this week as the upper \nlevel jet approaches the Great Lakes and then fixates through the \nworkweek. At the surface, a chain of surface low pressures is \nanchored to a stationary front extending from the High Plains to the \nUpper Midwest. A shortwave trough will eject from a deep upper low \nstuck over Nevada tonight to establish a primary surface wave that \nwill reach Lower Michigan on Tuesday. Meanwhile, a 594 dam 500 mb \nridge of high pressure situated over the Gulf Coast supports \ncontinued anticyclonic warm/moist advection into the region. \nMoisture metrics are already on the rise this afternoon with most \nASOS/AWOS sites reporting 60+ dewpoints. This ensures a mild night \nwith overnight lows in the mid (perhaps upper) 60s, or about 20F \nabove climatological normals. Before the low makes any progress into \nnorthern Lower, isentropic upsloping produces an uptick in mid \ncolumn saturation (700-500 mb) while drier air resides within an \nelevated warm layer (925-800 mb). Scattered overnight showers will \npartially fill in overnight, and remain elevated. Forecast soundings \nshow some elevated instability, maximizing at around 200 J/kg, so \nsome rumbles of thunder cannot be ruled out. No severe weather \nconcerns given the lack of CAPE density for severe hail and near-\nsurface stability to dampen the wind gust response.\n\nCAMs show conditions drying out pretty quickly Tuesday morning given \nhow quickly low and mid level moisture is stripped out, after flow \nveers WNW. The main forecast consideration will be temperature \ntrends for the rest of the day with 925 mb temperatures starting out \nin the low 20s (Celsius). Skies should also clear out for the peak \ndiurnal heating cycle, adding to the insolation potential. Models \ndiffer in timing the arrival of the primary cold front, but the more \ndelayed the boundary is, the higher the possible max temperature \nwill be. The latest NBM 5th percentile has 80s across most of Metro \nDetroit, so NWP confidence is high for another day with highs of \n80F. The record high for DTW on October 13 is within reach at 83F in \n1975. Seasonably mild Tuesday night behind the front, although still \na 20+ degree nocturnal drop, even with thickening cloud cover.\n\nThe cold front looks to stall out near the Michigan/Ohio border \nTuesday night and Wednesday as the jet axis approaches aloft. \nNumerous shortwave interactions promote a perturbed geopotential \nheight field offering additional chances for precipitation. This \nwill be augmented by the Gulf ridge which will still be in place, \nrenewing theta-e advection. Limited CAPE, but non-zero leaves a \nchance for a rumble of thunder. Any shower activity that continues \ninto the daylight hours will be brief as shortwave ridging builds in \nfrom the west, and flow flips NNE, dislodging the boundary. This \nalso allows a more seasonable airmass to filter in from Ontario. \nCold advection persists into Thursday, before the next wave of low \npressure lifts into southern Lower Thursday evening or during the \novernight hours. This brings another round of rain and a cold front. \nCooler and drier conditions develop Friday, lasting through the \nweekend.",
